WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.496 --> 00:00:36.817
In the burial ground of Saqqara in Egypt,

00:00:37.557 --> 00:00:39.057
30 kilometers south of Cairo,

00:00:39.798 --> 00:00:42.499
stands the pyramid complex of the Pharaoh Josiah,

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:44.760
built in 2700 BC.

00:01:06.536 --> 00:01:07.036
Egypt,

00:01:07.637 --> 00:01:08.698
5,000 years ago.

00:01:09.959 --> 00:01:10.739
The Nile Valley,

00:01:10.838 --> 00:01:12.340
the backbone of a great kingdom,

00:01:12.781 --> 00:01:14.882
born of the conquest of the north by the south,

00:01:15.382 --> 00:01:17.884
prospering from this narrow land of fertile land,

00:01:18.284 --> 00:01:19.385
irrigated by the river.

00:01:20.385 --> 00:01:21.807
This is the land of the living.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:27.501
In the west,

00:01:27.781 --> 00:01:29.201
the land of the dead begins.

00:01:29.942 --> 00:01:31.444
The plateau of the Libyan desert.

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:34.066
A huge cemetery overhanging the valley.

00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:36.948
Safe from the seasonal floods of the Nile.

00:01:40.831 --> 00:01:43.051
That is where all the pharaohs of Egypt lie,

00:01:43.313 --> 00:01:44.793
in gigantic sepulchres.

00:01:45.514 --> 00:01:46.234
The pyramids.

00:01:49.652 --> 00:01:50.772
Over 2,000 years,

00:01:50.832 --> 00:01:54.033
more than 70 were erected from the north to the south of the valley,

00:01:54.734 --> 00:01:56.295
all of them on the edge of the desert,

00:01:56.555 --> 00:01:57.295
west of the river.

00:01:59.816 --> 00:02:03.438
The model was invented at the start of the third millennium here at Saqqara,

00:02:04.058 --> 00:02:05.920
the necropolis of the city of Memphis,

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:08.061
which was then the capital of the kingdom.

00:02:09.281 --> 00:02:11.062
The pyramid of the Pharaoh Josiah,

00:02:11.462 --> 00:02:12.683
the first that was ever built.

00:02:19.532 --> 00:02:21.614
Ten times higher than any former sepulchre,

00:02:22.153 --> 00:02:25.456
it is 60 metres high in six stories that give it its name,

00:02:25.597 --> 00:02:26.737
the Step Pyramid.

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:36.566
It was explored for the first time in 1821 by a Prussian general and then by English and German Egyptologists.

00:02:38.527 --> 00:02:39.067
But in the early

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:39.989
20th century,

00:02:40.288 --> 00:02:42.951
a spectacular discovery threw everything into question.

00:02:44.012 --> 00:02:46.713
It had been thought that the pyramid was an isolated monument,

00:02:47.073 --> 00:02:51.917
but in reality an aerial photograph showed that it was at the centre of an enormous rectangle.

00:02:55.678 --> 00:02:59.401
The raised ruins of a complex of monuments built for the dead pharaoh,

00:02:59.901 --> 00:03:00.902
temples and a palace,

00:03:01.022 --> 00:03:02.103
rose from the sand.

00:03:06.391 --> 00:03:16.960
The French archaeologist Jean-Philippe Loer devoted the whole of his life to deciphering the layout and rebuilding fragments of it on the base courses and the scattered stones found on the site.

00:03:19.062 --> 00:03:24.367
Everything to be seen today standing more than a metre above the ground is the result of his reconstruction work.

00:03:26.689 --> 00:03:32.454
This part of a wall is just a small fragment of a 10 metre high boundary wall that originally enclosed the pyramid.

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:38.135
a rectangle 545 meters long and 277 wide,

00:03:38.655 --> 00:03:40.136
an area of 15 hectares.

00:03:43.058 --> 00:03:43.858
At the entrance,

00:03:44.078 --> 00:03:51.201
Loa discovered the remains of a covered colonnade forming a sort of vestibule that opened onto a large esplanade to the south of the pyramid.

00:03:52.082 --> 00:03:53.123
The esplanade is empty,

00:03:53.784 --> 00:03:55.745
unlike a smaller courtyard just beside it,

00:03:56.626 --> 00:03:58.907
where the ruins of 25 chapels were found.

00:04:01.063 --> 00:04:09.588
Large galleries inside the enclosure on the west and north sides were probably storerooms to hold supplies of food and clothes for the dead pharaoh's needs.

00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:20.196
Other traces indicate the existence of two huge chapels and a funerary temple to the north of the pyramid protecting the access to the tomb.

00:04:23.583 --> 00:04:27.284
Only a few pieces of this gigantic jigsaw puzzle are apparent today,

00:04:28.164 --> 00:04:33.526
but they are enough to reveal an architectural complex that is unique in both its ambition and its invention.

00:04:40.048 --> 00:04:44.430
The feet of the master of the house set on the plinth of a statue found near to the pyramid.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.050
The Pharao José,

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.191
the founder of the third dynasty.

00:04:49.711 --> 00:04:54.413
The front of the plinth bears his name as an incarnation of the falcon-headed god Horus.

00:04:56.734 --> 00:04:59.555
Beside it is the name of the oldest known architect,

00:05:00.816 --> 00:05:01.316
Imhotep,

00:05:01.776 --> 00:05:02.897
Pharaoh's chancellor,

00:05:03.097 --> 00:05:04.377
the inventor of the pyramid.

00:05:07.619 --> 00:05:10.120
There are no documents to tell us what gave him the idea,

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:11.801
nor why he chose this shape,

00:05:12.521 --> 00:05:16.002
but analysis of the construction shows that it was not achieved in one go.

00:05:17.611 --> 00:05:18.111
In fact,

00:05:18.172 --> 00:05:23.797
discoveries at the base of the pyramid showed traces of an earlier edifice in the shape of previous royal sepulchres.

00:05:24.938 --> 00:05:25.719
The construction,

00:05:25.779 --> 00:05:27.100
only a few meters high,

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:28.601
is known as a mastaba.

00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:32.665
There are many of them in the Saqqara necropolis,

00:05:33.145 --> 00:05:35.007
like this one that was recently restored.

00:05:40.012 --> 00:05:42.514
The architect started with a simple square mastaba.

00:05:45.739 --> 00:05:47.720
No doubt considering that to be too modest,

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.061
he enlarged it once,

00:05:49.481 --> 00:05:50.701
raising it to three levels,

00:05:51.342 --> 00:05:52.802
making a four-step pyramid.

00:05:54.983 --> 00:05:56.104
But that was still not enough.

00:05:57.284 --> 00:05:58.365
So work began again.

00:05:59.085 --> 00:06:00.225
The base was enlarged,

00:06:00.566 --> 00:06:01.806
and two more levels were added.

00:06:04.695 --> 00:06:07.096
That is how this unprecedented tomb was invented.

00:06:07.476 --> 00:06:10.218
A monumental signal on the same scale as the surroundings.

00:06:10.338 --> 00:06:12.298
Apart from the pride of its royal sponsor,

00:06:12.619 --> 00:06:15.460
this was certainly also a new conception of the power of the monarchy.

00:06:26.945 --> 00:06:29.306
The ancient mastabas were made from mud bricks,

00:06:29.406 --> 00:06:30.667
fragile and perishable.

00:06:32.351 --> 00:06:34.833
Imhotep chose dressed stone for his pyramid.

00:06:36.074 --> 00:06:37.015
According to legend,

00:06:37.195 --> 00:06:38.236
he was its inventor.

00:06:38.756 --> 00:06:39.137
In fact,

00:06:39.137 --> 00:06:41.258
dressed stone had been in use for several centuries,

00:06:41.679 --> 00:06:44.641
but it was employed sparingly for isolated elements of an edifice.

00:06:46.142 --> 00:06:52.768
Imhotep was the first to use it on such a large scale as the exclusive material for a funerary monument intended for eternity.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:55.690
Today,

00:06:55.690 --> 00:07:00.454
the steppe period of Saqqara is the world's most ancient monument in dressed stone.

00:07:05.487 --> 00:07:07.929
The limestone blocks were hewn from a nearby quarry.

00:07:08.209 --> 00:07:10.992
They are modestly sized and could be carried by one man alone.

00:07:13.914 --> 00:07:16.677
To ensure the stability of a structure 60 metres high,

00:07:16.817 --> 00:07:18.358
unprecedented at that time,

00:07:19.359 --> 00:07:22.722
the stones were laid in layers sloping at about 16 degrees,

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:27.286
like a succession of walls leaning on each other right to the central core.

00:07:32.899 --> 00:07:35.663
The edifice was raised piece by piece and layer by layer.

00:07:37.285 --> 00:07:39.008
A huge task because of its size,

00:07:39.428 --> 00:07:41.792
but one that did not call for any extraordinary equipment.

00:07:43.053 --> 00:07:43.654
In that sense,

00:07:43.674 --> 00:07:44.996
the pyramid has no mystery.

00:07:51.883 --> 00:07:52.864
Its appearance today,

00:07:53.284 --> 00:07:53.784
rugged,

00:07:54.244 --> 00:07:54.865
rock-strewn,

00:07:55.225 --> 00:07:56.646
and rather like a heap of stones,

00:07:56.706 --> 00:07:57.426
is deceptive.

00:07:58.387 --> 00:07:58.887
Originally,

00:07:58.907 --> 00:08:04.450
the pyramid was completely covered by a magnificent smooth facing of white limestone from the quarries at Tora,

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:06.071
on the other bank of the Nile.

00:08:08.332 --> 00:08:08.792
Today,

00:08:08.792 --> 00:08:10.273
little of this facing remains.

00:08:12.314 --> 00:08:13.755
Dismantled over the centuries,

00:08:14.135 --> 00:08:17.117
the stones were used in building mosques and palaces in Cairo,

00:08:17.837 --> 00:08:19.438
leaving these skinned remains here,

00:08:19.758 --> 00:08:20.779
a flayed pyramid.

00:08:28.972 --> 00:08:32.134
The Djoser pyramid was to be the first of a very long series,

00:08:32.634 --> 00:08:41.017
but during the next dynasty they had the idea of filling in the steps to make a continuous line and thus creating the classic smooth-sided pyramid.

00:08:42.038 --> 00:08:43.839
But that too did not happen all at once.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.681
One of the early attempts was at Dashur,

00:08:48.901 --> 00:08:50.481
30 kilometres south of Saqqara,

00:08:50.962 --> 00:08:52.822
the so-called rhomboidal pyramid.

00:08:54.711 --> 00:08:55.312
At the start,

00:08:55.332 --> 00:08:58.795
the slope was too steep and had to be rectified in the course of construction.

00:09:01.498 --> 00:09:02.699
Disappointed with the result,

00:09:02.839 --> 00:09:05.942
the Pharos Nefru had a second one built just beside it.

00:09:09.185 --> 00:09:09.646
This time,

00:09:09.646 --> 00:09:10.746
the base was too wide,

00:09:11.007 --> 00:09:14.630
and the pyramid seems to be flattened in spite of being 105 metres high.

00:09:19.659 --> 00:09:21.101
Unlike the pyramids built later,

00:09:22.183 --> 00:09:27.370
the Djoser pyramid is nothing more than a gigantic solid tombstone with neither corridors nor vault.

00:09:31.175 --> 00:09:33.499
The Pharaoh's tomb was discovered beneath the pyramid.

00:09:34.041 --> 00:09:34.541
Dug out,

00:09:34.782 --> 00:09:36.463
28 metres below ground level,

00:09:36.763 --> 00:09:40.106
was an extraordinary network of galleries around a granite vault.

00:09:41.527 --> 00:09:44.790
These underground funerary spaces are unique in Egyptian history.

00:09:45.511 --> 00:09:46.792
They are inaccessible today.

00:09:48.913 --> 00:09:55.299
But Imhotep twinned this network of galleries situated under the pyramid with a second network near to the southern enclosure.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:57.761
It is the pharaoh's second tomb.

00:10:04.177 --> 00:10:06.378
No one knows the exact reason for this doubling up,

00:10:06.738 --> 00:10:13.101
but this southern enclosure tomb is at the same depth as the one under the pyramid and reproduces it in both decorations and plan.

00:10:14.282 --> 00:10:15.022
In both cases,

00:10:15.082 --> 00:10:18.144
the underground galleries have storehouses stocked with food,

00:10:18.824 --> 00:10:23.086
crockery and clothing that the defunct pharaoh would need during the hereafter.

00:10:25.867 --> 00:10:26.547
But a pharaoh,

00:10:26.687 --> 00:10:27.468
even when dead,

00:10:27.808 --> 00:10:29.209
is nothing without his palace.

00:10:30.265 --> 00:10:34.267
So here Imhotep built a reduced model of the pharaoh's early possessions.

00:10:51.794 --> 00:10:55.876
At
the heart of the complex is a central corridor that opens onto two passages,

00:10:56.236 --> 00:10:57.077
one on each side.

00:11:00.709 --> 00:11:11.014
On the wall of the left-hand passage represented facades of the chapels with their arch roofs supported by what was called Jed pillars that symbolise the duration and stability of the universe.

00:11:19.817 --> 00:11:22.979
The symmetrical passage figures the facade of the royal palace.

00:11:23.759 --> 00:11:28.021
The remains of a splendid covering of blue earthenware tiles imitating reed mats

00:11:29.017 --> 00:11:31.878
Frame the doors of the palace which held the pharaoh himself.

00:11:43.801 --> 00:11:46.102
These doors are represented as being wide open.

00:11:46.602 --> 00:11:49.163
That is a meaning of this reproduction of a rolled up mat.

00:11:52.104 --> 00:11:55.645
The same symbolic opening is shown in the little windows and above the doors.

00:11:59.089 --> 00:12:01.570
Behind this facade of the palace seen from outside,

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:04.812
other rooms were built for the pharaoh to retire into.

00:12:06.372 --> 00:12:07.153
Still the palace,

00:12:07.453 --> 00:12:08.593
but seen from inside.

00:12:17.017 --> 00:12:17.517
On the wall,

00:12:18.018 --> 00:12:19.238
windows in bas-relief,

00:12:19.398 --> 00:12:20.079
quite simple.

00:12:21.639 --> 00:12:24.441
The dead pharaoh is at home here in his afterlife.

00:12:49.225 --> 00:12:50.265
A line of cobras,

00:12:50.606 --> 00:12:52.527
protective divinities of northern Egypt,

00:12:53.187 --> 00:12:54.627
guards the king's second tomb.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:01.211
On this fragment of wall,

00:13:01.651 --> 00:13:02.691
in simplified form,

00:13:03.231 --> 00:13:05.352
is the same decor as the underground palace.

00:13:06.293 --> 00:13:06.813
Windows,

00:13:07.093 --> 00:13:07.593
doors,

00:13:07.633 --> 00:13:09.414
rolled-up mats inviting you to enter.

00:13:10.815 --> 00:13:14.156
But the architectural pretense takes on another dimension for all to see.

00:13:15.437 --> 00:13:16.998
It is on the scale of a real city,

00:13:17.298 --> 00:13:18.899
designed as a petrified replica.

00:13:34.287 --> 00:13:40.371
The
great enclosure with its 211 bastions was probably modelled on the real enclosure of the royal city of Memphis,

00:13:41.011 --> 00:13:43.052
famous for its legendary mud brick walls.

00:13:44.733 --> 00:13:46.814
whose appearance is carefully reproduced here.

00:13:47.795 --> 00:13:55.799
Even these little rectangles sculpted in the dressed stone imitate the ends of wooden beams that were put into real brick walls to strengthen their upper parts.

00:14:00.501 --> 00:14:02.902
The presence in the surround of 14 mock doors,

00:14:03.222 --> 00:14:05.083
of which only one is a real entrance,

00:14:05.384 --> 00:14:07.985
is a further indication of faithfulness to the original.

00:14:17.006 --> 00:14:20.169
The entrance is oddly small and has no system for closing it.

00:14:27.275 --> 00:14:28.697
The doors are figured further on.

00:14:30.818 --> 00:14:36.464
At the opening of a long passage crossing the whole thickness of the enclosure are hinges sculpted in the stone,

00:14:36.844 --> 00:14:38.765
along with the outline of the great doors,

00:14:39.226 --> 00:14:40.187
opened for eternity.

00:14:55.587 --> 00:14:56.708
In the underground palace,

00:14:56.728 --> 00:14:58.329
Djoser is often depicted running.

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:01.491
This shows the ceremony of Hebzed,

00:15:01.691 --> 00:15:05.653
a ritual in which the living sovereign is running to prove that he has the strength to reign.

00:15:08.655 --> 00:15:11.357
By continuing to celebrate Hebzed in the afterlife,

00:15:11.637 --> 00:15:14.139
the dead pharaoh retains his powers for eternity.

00:15:16.802 --> 00:15:18.745
Below the ground he can only run in effigy.

00:15:19.125 --> 00:15:22.728
In the open air he was provided with a real space for this sacred exercise.

00:15:23.248 --> 00:15:24.971
The esplanade to the south of the pyramid,

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:31.596
where stand the base of two large boundary stones marking the limits of the track and probably symbolizing the limits of the kingdom.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:39.583
After the trial the ceremony continued in the neighboring courtyard,

00:15:39.983 --> 00:15:42.226
where the remains of 25 chapels were unearthed.

00:15:43.694 --> 00:15:46.216
This is the largest architectural complex on the site.

00:15:58.024 --> 00:16:01.927
The chapels reproduce the typical architecture of the two kingdoms that made up Egypt,

00:16:02.248 --> 00:16:03.408
on the west of the courtyard,

00:16:03.588 --> 00:16:04.408
the southern kingdom,

00:16:04.649 --> 00:16:05.210
on the east,

00:16:05.429 --> 00:16:06.311
the northern kingdom.

00:16:11.995 --> 00:16:18.940
Thus the deceased pharaoh can pay homage to all the gods of the two kingdoms before being reinstalled in his powers and twice crowned anew.

00:16:20.522 --> 00:16:22.783
As a sovereign of the north and a sovereign of the south,

00:16:23.124 --> 00:16:26.526
Onidaeus served by two flights of steps in supporting a double throne.

00:16:32.182 --> 00:16:33.102
In front of the chapels,

00:16:33.122 --> 00:16:38.105
the architect provided access to a kind of chican with low walls and open to the sky.

00:16:39.365 --> 00:16:40.605
With their defensive aspect,

00:16:40.805 --> 00:16:43.326
they seem to be protecting the approach to the chapels.

00:16:44.528 --> 00:16:44.908
However,

00:16:44.908 --> 00:16:46.988
the stone doors folded back and the wide,

00:16:47.088 --> 00:16:47.509
open,

00:16:47.509 --> 00:16:51.930
carved wood barriers inviting entrance suggest the opposite.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:17:03.537
These little walls are in fact a means of materialising the ritual path that leads to the niches holding statues of the pharaoh,

00:17:04.317 --> 00:17:05.057
and stops there.

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:08.361
Because there is no way into the chapels,

00:17:08.481 --> 00:17:09.142
they are solid,

00:17:09.442 --> 00:17:10.923
without any interior space.

00:17:12.684 --> 00:17:17.309
Behind the polished stone facades they are filled with nothing but rocks and gravel.

00:17:22.542 --> 00:17:24.003
They are make-believe buildings,

00:17:24.484 --> 00:17:26.225
a full-size stage set,

00:17:26.485 --> 00:17:30.809
reproducing only the facades of the temporary chapels erected for the ceremony of Hebzed,

00:17:32.171 --> 00:17:34.072
as it was practiced by the living pharaoh.

00:17:35.653 --> 00:17:36.615
In the world of the dead,

00:17:36.794 --> 00:17:37.955
appearances suffice.

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:45.841
All the decorative elements here are signs and symbols.

00:17:46.658 --> 00:17:52.024
These columns with their papyrus form guarding the suggestion of a door represent the Nile Delta.

00:17:54.367 --> 00:17:57.810
But they are also petrified glimpses of the architecture of the living world,

00:17:59.312 --> 00:18:01.014
an architecture of earth and wood,

00:18:01.735 --> 00:18:04.117
perishable materials imitated in stone.

00:18:06.367 --> 00:18:10.530
Such as the ceilings of the entrance corridor and some other rough-hewn interior spaces,

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:17.554
the great blocks of stone laid on their sides are sculpted in the round to give the appearance of ceilings made of logs.

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:30.883
The facades of the chapels also imitate the traditional forms of vegetal architecture.

00:18:31.623 --> 00:18:34.526
Their arched roofs copy those of reed-built cabins,

00:18:34.926 --> 00:18:36.868
curved to give greater resistance.

00:18:41.452 --> 00:18:43.433
As to the columns that ornament the façade,

00:18:43.634 --> 00:18:45.575
they are like tree trunks holding up the roof,

00:18:46.136 --> 00:18:51.080
while the rectangular capitals represent the ends of horizontal beams supported by the uprights,

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:54.303
as can still be seen in the Egyptian countryside today.

00:19:05.691 --> 00:19:11.353
Columns of the same type embellished the facades of the two large chapels to the north and south of the Hebzid courtyard.

00:19:11.793 --> 00:19:13.374
According to Loew's calculations,

00:19:13.413 --> 00:19:16.975
they were 12 metres high and large coloured banners were hung from the summits,

00:19:17.335 --> 00:19:19.656
floating in the breeze to witness the presence of the gods.

00:19:21.596 --> 00:19:24.418
It's a difficult sight to imagine when looking at what remains today.

00:19:35.022 --> 00:19:38.549
These columns against the façade symbolize the continuity of the tradition.

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:42.981
In the large entrance colonnade,

00:19:42.982 --> 00:19:43.721
on the other hand,

00:19:44.202 --> 00:19:45.622
there is a real revolution.

00:19:49.865 --> 00:19:52.566
The 40 great columns here have no known precedent.

00:19:54.347 --> 00:19:58.129
This is the first appearance of a style of column that has now become familiar to us,

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:01.872
a form that was to mark architecture for millennia to come.

00:20:10.224 --> 00:20:14.629
The columns are made of piled-up drums with the relief carved after assembly.

00:20:16.992 --> 00:20:18.974
They too are inspired by vegetation.

00:20:19.835 --> 00:20:24.000
They imitate the stems of reeds or palms tied into bundles,

00:20:24.241 --> 00:20:26.223
hence the name bundled reed columns.

00:20:31.464 --> 00:20:34.028
The drums making up the columns are fitted into the walls,

00:20:34.129 --> 00:20:35.411
which ensures their stability,

00:20:35.912 --> 00:20:39.538
at the same time creating niches to house the statues of the dead pharaoh.

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:48.700
Their autonomy was still relative,

00:20:49.161 --> 00:20:51.261
but the move was away from the decorative column,

00:20:51.501 --> 00:20:53.122
a simple form stuck to the wall,

00:20:53.722 --> 00:20:57.003
to the structural column supporting a lintel and ceiling,

00:20:57.643 --> 00:21:00.584
part of the structure no longer merely flattened decoration,

00:21:00.864 --> 00:21:02.104
but a volume in space.

00:21:13.387 --> 00:21:15.808
Did the builder realise what threshold he was crossing?

00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:20.221
The columns and walls bear traces of paint,

00:21:21.042 --> 00:21:21.942
black for the walls,

00:21:22.422 --> 00:21:23.283
red for the columns.

00:21:24.183 --> 00:21:26.225
A way of hiding the walls that were still needed,

00:21:27.305 --> 00:21:29.547
to make them appear to be completely freestanding.

00:21:38.111 --> 00:21:42.414
This scrap of stone found here is the oldest architectural sketch ever found.

00:21:43.995 --> 00:21:45.416
The calculations for a vault.

00:21:52.856 --> 00:21:54.397
No other document has been found,

00:21:54.837 --> 00:21:58.519
no text relative to the construction of the pyramid or the surrounding buildings.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:01.461
But the perfect alignment of the walls,

00:22:01.541 --> 00:22:06.524
the regulation of the brick courses and the attention to detail show that at the beginning of the third millennium,

00:22:06.924 --> 00:22:12.487
Egypt entered into a period of technical and cultural innovation linked to the development of stone-built architecture.

00:22:15.649 --> 00:22:17.811
Everything here speaks of unprecedented mastery,

00:22:18.411 --> 00:22:19.211
and of pleasure too.

00:22:20.328 --> 00:22:24.631
The architect shows his virtuosity by the geometric perfection of his use of dressed stone,

00:22:25.332 --> 00:22:30.796
here demonstrated by the astonishing curved wall that leads from the courtyard of the Heb Sed ceremony towards another temple,

00:22:30.997 --> 00:22:33.158
one that is quite different from all the other buildings.

00:22:45.949 --> 00:22:49.311
At the centre of this temple there is a little room whose ceiling has disappeared.

00:22:52.592 --> 00:22:53.353
When it was covered,

00:22:53.354 --> 00:22:54.814
it was lighted by daylight,

00:22:54.954 --> 00:22:57.916
let in through carved openings in the lintel of the Jed Pillars,

00:22:58.076 --> 00:22:59.156
sculpted in the lintel.

00:23:01.618 --> 00:23:02.479
An inspiration,

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:03.840
an architectural statement,

00:23:03.841 --> 00:23:04.800
as we might say today,

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:08.743
associating sunlight with the symbols of the stability of the kingdom and the world.

00:23:13.626 --> 00:23:14.687
As with the other buildings,

00:23:14.747 --> 00:23:16.928
we do not know the exact purpose of the temple.

00:23:17.869 --> 00:23:21.551
What sets it apart from the others is simply that it has real internal spaces.

00:23:22.512 --> 00:23:26.075
While the dead pharaoh can be content with blind facades and a sculpted decor,

00:23:26.495 --> 00:23:31.078
in these rooms that can be entered and walked through there is something that is more suitable for the living.

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:47.390
A
ruin.

00:23:48.391 --> 00:23:49.091
A laboratory.

00:23:51.196 --> 00:23:55.279
They tried here to transpose the forms inherited from tradition using new materials,

00:23:55.579 --> 00:23:57.140
so as to keep them safe for eternity.

00:23:58.201 --> 00:24:00.703
And from this desire for imitation came new forms,

00:24:00.823 --> 00:24:01.984
a new art of building.

00:24:04.605 --> 00:24:06.967
This revolution took place in a funerary monument.

00:24:07.527 --> 00:24:08.948
This is certainly not by chance.

00:24:10.409 --> 00:24:18.675
Let's pretend a representation removed from the practical problems of ordinary mortals gave place to what today we call architecture.

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:22.660
At least,

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:24.001
that is the way we look at things.

00:24:25.742 --> 00:24:27.383
For those who built this city in stone,

00:24:27.423 --> 00:24:28.603
the words imitation,

00:24:29.084 --> 00:24:29.584
decor,

00:24:29.724 --> 00:24:31.705
representation make no sense,

00:24:32.405 --> 00:24:36.207
no more than the boundary that is so obvious to us between reality and its symbol.

00:24:38.529 --> 00:24:38.889
For them,

00:24:38.889 --> 00:24:43.671
the statues of the pharaoh and his family were really and truly the pharaoh and his family.

00:24:51.984 --> 00:24:57.346
This little stone bunker adjoining the north base of the pyramid is one of the main attractions of the site today.

00:24:58.686 --> 00:25:01.367
The tourists crowd round to look through these two holes,

00:25:01.787 --> 00:25:06.549
persuaded that Imhotep put them there so that the living could look inside to see the statue of the Pharaoh.

00:25:11.650 --> 00:25:12.471
Quite the contrary.

00:25:13.611 --> 00:25:15.131
These holes were not made for us,

00:25:15.612 --> 00:25:16.132
but for him.

00:25:17.624 --> 00:25:18.885
On the other side of the stone,

00:25:19.085 --> 00:25:19.945
night after night,

00:25:20.205 --> 00:25:23.686
the dead pharaoh looks outwards to where the pole star shines.

